====================================================================================
PART 1 -- THE TOP-r-COEFFICIENT IDENTITY (the rigorous algebraic core)
====================================================================================
 CLAIM: for P(r,u) = sum_q g_q(r) u^q, D = max_q deg_r g_q, and leading symbol
        Lam(u) = sum_{deg g_q = D} lc(g_q) u^q,  we have  [r^{mD}] CT_u(P^m) = CT_u(Lam^m).
 PROOF: CT_u(P^m) = sum_{q_1+...+q_m=0} prod_i g_{q_i}(r); a product has r-degree mD iff
        every q_i is a top-degree charge, and then its [r^{mD}] coeff is prod lc(g_{q_i}).

   P charges [-1, 0, 1], D=1: [r^{mD}]CT(P^m) = CT(Lam^m) for m=2,3,4 : all match
   P charges [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2], D=2: [r^{mD}]CT(P^m) = CT(Lam^m) for m=2,3,4 : all match
   P charges [-1, 0, 1], D=3: [r^{mD}]CT(P^m) = CT(Lam^m) for m=2,3,4 : all match

   IDENTITY HOLDS on every control: True

====================================================================================
PART 2 -- DOMINATION FAILS: independent check of MISTAKE-202, retracting my S351 mechanism
====================================================================================
 P = Zb + 1 + Z  (alpha = r, beta = 1); E[P^m] known nonzero.  Track |top term|/sum|terms|
 of E_r[L_m] = sum_k ([r^k]L_m) k!  as m grows:

      m         E[P^m]    |top term|/sum|terms|
      2              3                   0.6667
      6            331                   0.3625
     12        3609673                   0.1843
     18   163716695587                   0.1078
     24 18983329135883665                   0.0682

   CONFIRMED: the top term's fraction COLLAPSES (matches death-star's 0.068 at m=24).  So the
   ">50% mass / top term dominates" mechanism is FALSE -- and that was exactly the mechanism my
   klein-S351 Gamma bridge invoked.  I RETRACT THE MECHANISM.  The bridge's CONCLUSION
   (E[P^m] != 0) is still true here, but NOT because the top term dominates.  Something else
   keeps it nonzero.  Part 3 identifies what.


====================================================================================
PART 3 -- POSITIVITY is the real mechanism on the radial {-1,+1} span
====================================================================================
 On the {-1,+1} span (beta = 0, charge-0 empty), the {-1,0,1} GF collapses:
   E[P^{2j}] = C(2j,j) E_r[alpha^j],  alpha = r * a(r) * c(r).
 For P in C[Z,Zb] the charge +1 and -1 coefficients are conjugate, so a*c = |c|^2 >= 0,
 whence alpha = r|c|^2 >= 0 pointwise and E_r[alpha^j] > 0 unless c == 0.  NO DOMINATION
 NEEDED -- every moment is a positive number, so it cannot vanish.  Exhibited:

   c=1: alpha = r*c^2, E_r[alpha^j] for j=1..4 = [1, 2, 6, 24]  (all > 0: True)
   c=1+r: alpha = r*c^2, E_r[alpha^j] for j=1..4 = [11, 1370, 696750, 923389464]  (all > 0: True)
   c=r^2-3 (has a sign change!): alpha = r*c^2, E_r[alpha^j] for j=1..4 = [93, 3181410, 1200786759414, 2284388436083583384]  (all > 0: True)

   Note c = r^2 - 3 CHANGES SIGN, yet alpha = r c^2 >= 0 regardless -- the SQUARE is what
   matters, not c.  So positivity holds for ALL c, no degree bound, matching opus THM-1535/1540
   (Hankel positive-definiteness) and boxeph's radial route.  This is the correct mechanism,
   and it is immune to the domination collapse of Part 2 because it never compares terms.

====================================================================================
PART 4 -- WHY THE BOTH-SIGNS {-1,0,1} CASE ESCAPES POSITIVITY (the precise gap)
====================================================================================
 With beta != 0, L_m(alpha,beta) = sum_k C(...) alpha^k beta^{m-2k} is NO LONGER a
 positive combination: beta^{m-2k} alternates in sign when beta < 0, and even for
 beta > 0 the r-integrand L_m(r) can take both signs.  Exhibited on alpha=r, beta=1-r:

   alpha=r, beta=1-r:
      m=2: L_m(r) coefficient signs = ['1']   E_r[L_m] = 3
      m=3: L_m(r) coefficient signs = ['-1', '1']   E_r[L_m] = -8
      m=4: L_m(r) coefficient signs = ['-1', '1']   E_r[L_m] = 57
      m=5: L_m(r) coefficient signs = ['-1', '1']   E_r[L_m] = -384

   So on the both-signs span the integrand L_m(r) is SIGN-INDEFINITE -- positivity is
   unavailable, and (Part 2) domination is false.  THE PRECISE REMAINING GAP for GMC(2):
   show E_r[L_m] != 0 for some m when (alpha,beta) is not the trivial (r|c|^2, 0), using
   NEITHER termwise positivity NOR top-term domination.  The known routes are opus's Hankel
   argument on the charge-0 sub-block and boxeph's pinch/Watson analysis; this file's
   contribution is to have (i) made the algebraic core rigorous (Part 1), (ii) killed the
   domination mechanism cleanly (Part 2, retracting my own S351), and (iii) isolated the gap
   as sign-indefiniteness of L_m rather than anything about growth rates (Part 4).

